<p>I like forums. Debates, questions, answers, opinions, etc. That feeling of an asynchronous community, where answers are thought through and content lasts, is what makes the internet an interesting place. That is why I like reading them and taking part in them. But I had never designed or built one. In my spare time I started sketching one, and I ended up building my humble <em>headless</em>, federated forum, with simple, modern technologies and open standards.</p>
<p>And I learned a lot along the way. A forum looks simple, but it hides the classic problems of any server. Who can do what? How do you tell a moderator from a regular user? What happens when you have to ban someone? How do you stop someone from stealing an account? Roles, permissions, bans and security. I had to solve them one by one, and you don't learn that by reading.</p>
<p>Let me tell you how I made <a href="https://git.andros.dev/andros/tyrtulia">Tyrtulia</a>.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--a-forum-with-no-interface" class="blog-single__content-title">A forum with no interface</h2>
<p>The server only offers a solid, documented API. It is REST with hypermedia: the client only knows one route, <code>GET /</code>, and discovers everything else by following links. Underneath there are some very boring standards:</p>
<ul>
<li>RFC 9110: HTTP semantics, with <code>ETag</code> and <code>If-Match</code> so edits don't overwrite each other.</li>
<li>RFC 9457: errors.</li>
<li>RFC 7396: partial updates with <code>PATCH</code>.</li>
<li>RFC 6570: URL templates.</li>
<li>RFC 4287: Atom feeds.</li>
<li>RFC 9562: UUIDv7 identifiers.</li>
<li>JSON Schema: validation.</li>
<li>Server-Sent Events: real-time events.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is no web interface, no forms, no advanced authentication systems, no file handling (images, attachments, etc.), and nothing that isn't strictly needed to read and write messages.</p>
<p>That is why it is so light. It uses between 60 and 70 MB of RAM and about 50 MB of disk. It only needs Python and SQLite to run.</p>
<h3 id="blog-single-content__title--an-api-that-discovers-itself" class="blog-single__content-title">An API that discovers itself</h3>
<p>How does a client know what it can do? The server tells it with links. Look at the same request, <code>GET /</code>, made by a guest (simplified):</p>
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json">{
    "name": "Forum of example.org",
    "_links": {
        "self": {"href": "/", "method": "GET"},
        "latest": {"href": "/threads", "method": "GET"},
        "search": {"href": "/search{?q,author,in}", "method": "GET", "templated": true},
        "login": {"href": "/sessions", "method": "POST", "schema": "/schemas/session"}
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>And now by <code>alice</code>, logged in:</p>
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json">{
    "name": "Forum of example.org",
    "_links": {
        "self": {"href": "/", "method": "GET"},
        "latest": {"href": "/threads", "method": "GET"},
        "search": {"href": "/search{?q,author,in}", "method": "GET", "templated": true},
        "createThread": {"href": "/threads", "method": "POST", "schema": "/schemas/thread"},
        "notifications": {"href": "/notifications", "method": "GET"},
        "events": {"href": "/events", "method": "GET", "type": "text/event-stream"}
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>If I change a route tomorrow, no client breaks, because none of them knew it in advance. They just follow the links the server gives them.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--why-not-use-one-that-already-exists" class="blog-single__content-title">Why not use one that already exists?</h2>
<p>I know there are very complete free software forums:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="https://www.discourse.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Discourse</a></strong>: the most popular one today.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://flarum.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Flarum</a></strong>: PHP with MySQL.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://nodebb.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">NodeBB</a></strong>: Node.js, with MongoDB, Redis or PostgreSQL. It stands out for real time with WebSockets.</li>
</ul>
<p>Or the good old classic: <a href="https://www.phpbb.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">phpBB</a>.</p>
<p>They are all fantastic, but they are designed to be used from a web browser. They also have an ambitious scope. That is ideal for large communities, but too much for a small, light forum.</p>
<p>That said, Tyrtulia doesn't give up on the browser: it can be indexed by search engines and anyone can read it on the web.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--no-passwords" class="blog-single__content-title">No passwords</h2>
<p>No OAuth, no OpenID, no sign-up forms. There are two ways to authenticate:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your Unix user</strong>: the client talks to the forum through a Unix socket. The operating system tells the forum which user is on the other side. No sign-up, no login. Your user is your account.</li>
<li><strong>SSH key</strong>: the client connects over HTTP and the forum sends it a challenge. The client signs it with your private SSH key and the forum verifies it with your public key.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is no registration over HTTP. If you have a Unix user on the server, you already have an account: it is created the first time you connect through the socket. From there you can add your SSH keys to log in from outside. If you don't have a user on the machine, the administrator creates an account for you with your public key from the command line. On a tilde, having a Unix user is already the invitation.</p>
<p>Easy to use, secure, and no passwords to steal.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--what-changes-compared-to-bbj" class="blog-single__content-title">What changes compared to BBJ</h2>
<p>The idea isn't mine. I was inspired by <a href="https://github.com/bbj-dev/bbj" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">BBJ</a>, a bulletin board for the terminal built for small communities or clubs (tildes, pubnixes, club servers, etc.). I took its spirit and brought it to my own ground. Still, there are important differences.</p>
<p>First, the foundations.</p>
