</div><p>What they share is a small server built on SQLite and several text clients against a documented API.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--clients" class="blog-single__content-title">Clients</h2>
<p>A <em>headless</em> forum needs clients, no way around it.</p>
<h3 id="blog-single-content__title--tyrtulia-tui" class="blog-single__content-title">Tyrtulia TUI</h3>
<p><a href="https://git.andros.dev/andros/tyrtulia-tui">Tyrtulia TUI</a> is the terminal client, built with Textual.</p>
<p><img alt="A thread in Tyrtulia TUI" src="https://img.andros.dev/a68h_x8D3VTyz8p6Y-BoFh0w2Gw=/filters:format(avif):quality(85)/https://andros.dev/media/blog/2026/10/tyrtulia-tui-thread.png" /></p>
<h3 id="blog-single-content__title--tyrtuliael" class="blog-single__content-title">tyrtulia.el</h3>
<p>If you know me a little, you know this package was a must: <a href="https://git.andros.dev/andros/tyrtulia.el">tyrtulia.el</a>.</p>
<p><img alt="A thread in tyrtulia.el" src="https://img.andros.dev/oDU8xw1h26NmhxlC25_EYvrLc3I=/filters:format(avif):quality(85)/https://andros.dev/media/blog/2026/10/tyrtulia-el-thread.png" /></p>
<h3 id="blog-single-content__title--tyrtulia-web" class="blog-single__content-title">Tyrtulia Web</h3>
<p><a href="https://git.andros.dev/andros/tyrtulia-web">Tyrtulia Web</a> is more a mirror of the content than a client. Its goal is to let search engines index the forum and let anyone read it from a browser.</p>
<p><img alt="A thread in Tyrtulia Web" src="https://img.andros.dev/dGNVvmXPwcmaRRWW0hQQh3SqU7Q=/filters:format(avif):quality(85)/https://andros.dev/media/blog/2026/10/tyrtulia-web-thread.png" /></p>
<p>It has an unstyled theme and another one that mimics my website.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--federation" class="blog-single__content-title">Federation</h2>
<p>You can connect to one forum or set up several. Federation happens in the clients: servers don't talk to each other or copy messages between them. Each forum is independent, with its own users, rules and moderators.</p>
<p>Instead of splitting them into tabs or accounts, the clients mix the threads of all of them into a single list.</p>
<p><img alt="Three forums in the same list" src="https://img.andros.dev/J9wYL7kquMLlj2nNlNjC1koCw0Y=/filters:format(avif):quality(85)/https://andros.dev/media/blog/2026/10/tyrtulia-el-forums.png" /></p>
<p>Of course, you can only write in the forums where you have an account. In the rest you can only read.</p>
<p>I think it's a pretty cool feature, because it keeps the API code much simpler and lets you explore other forums at zero cost.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--try-it" class="blog-single__content-title">Try it</h2>
<p>It has been fun to design and build. Of course there are more complete forums, more powerful federation systems, and even decentralized systems that leave Tyrtulia in the dust (Aether, Nostr...). But its niche is very specific: a light, closed-access, federated, indexable, secure forum with text clients. For everything else, there are better things out there.</p>
<p>If you feel like trying it, the installation guide is in the <a href="https://git.andros.dev/andros/tyrtulia#getting-started-for-administrators">Tyrtulia repository</a>.</p>
